          "crash"
        ],
        "rev": 1
      },
      "percussionGap": 32,
      "notes": "POLYMETER (9.1 recipe B): the noise weather runs a 5-row cell whose phase is carried across the whole of B and the peak - entry rows 0/1/2/3/4 at frames 4/5/6/7/8 (64-row frames: entry = (-64k) mod 5), cited 4:0 -> 8:59, five frames, one complete re-alignment cycle, and it stops exactly where A' begins. POLYRHYTHM 2:3 (recipe D): pulse 2 runs a 6-row dotted-quarter cell through the whole A section, entry rows 0/2/4 at frames 1/2/3, cited 1:0 -> 3:58 - two attacks per three beats against the 4-row beat. TRESILLO (recipe C, 16th level 3+3+2): the A' ripple at 9:0/3/6 + 9:8/11/14 and again at 9:32 and 10:0, which is ideal at this tempo. METRIC SURPRISE (recipe H): D param 0 at 8:59 ends the peak section a beat early so A' arrives displaced; the render is 700 rows, not 704. DISPLACEMENT (recipe F): the cell is restated +2 rows at 2:2, audible at 90 BPM. COUNTERPOINT: pulse 2 has its own rhythm and contour for the whole A section (the 6-row cell) and again at 6:4 -> 6:60, where every one of its eight attacks falls on a row pulse 1 does not touch. Written suspensions: 4:24 holds g4 from the borrowed Eb across the change to F at 4:32 and resolves down by step to f4 at 4:36; the cadential appoggiatura is at 10:32, f5 attacked on the strong beat over C and resolving down to e5 at 10:36. HARMONY, three distinct non-diatonic devices in three sections: (1) modal interchange - the borrowed b flat VII (E flat major) at 4:0 and 5:0, its e flat in the lead at 4:8 and its b flat in pulse 2 at 4:0, flatly contradicting lydian's raised fourth; (2) chromatic mediant - A major at 8:32 against F major's F-A-C, sharing only a, its c sharp 5 voiced in pulse 2 at 8:32 and resolving down a semitone to c5 at 8:48; (3) chromatic bass descent - the triangle walks f1-e1-eb1-d1, one link per bar, 9:0 -> 9:48, in its lowest octave, with the pulses holding so the harmony is implied by the collision. Harmonic rhythm changes per section: A and B move one chord per two bars, 6:0 doubles it to one per bar, and the peak holds one chord for a whole frame. THE BRIEFED TECHNIQUES: long macro envelopes with real release points - pad (vol-pad, release 11) and lead-thin (vol-lead, release 5) - and every phrase ends with === so the release tails overlap the next entry (1:28, 1:44, 1:60 and so on). Axy swells: A param 4 = A04, a slow swell UP (the y digit is the up direction) at 0:0 on both pulses and again on the peak note at 7:0, cancelled with A param 0 at 0:32 and 7:24. Pxx static detune: P param 130 = 0x82 = two raw period units sharp, set on pulse 2 at 0:0 and RE-STATED at the loop target 1:0 so the seam state does not depend on the intro; the two pulses beat slowly wherever they touch the same pitch, which is deliberate at 5:0 where pulse 2 doubles the lead in unison. The one destabilisation: 4 param 247 = 4f7, speed 15 depth 7, at 8:0 on the held global peak d6 - earned by five frames of stillness - cancelled with 4 param 0 at 8:12, after which the note resolves down by step. 7 param 50 = 732 tremolo on the peak pad at 7:16, cancelled with 7 param 0 at 8:48 so nothing runs across the seam. B param 1 = loop to frame 1. NO DRUM KIT, and percussionGap 32 is declared for it: the noise lane is weather, not percussion - crash (note 46, index 1) as a 24-tick wash and hat-closed (note 45, index 2) as drips - so it has no backbeat to keep and its longest silence is 31 rows, at the intro-to-A seam. rmsRange: measured -22.82 dBFS across the two-pass render; the design's [-20,-9] window is unreachable on this engine, so the declared window is a snug band around the measurement, above the -30 floor. The triangle sustains through every transition including the loop seam because the render gate fails on any 1.2 s window below -60 dBFS and this piece has no kit to carry it."
